course: dictionary
lesson: 7929
title: Ready and its meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ready has more than one meaning, and the words around it show you which one. «So he has just 5 minutes to get ready.» Here get ready means prepare, like putting on your shoes and packing your bag. Ready means prepared and able to do something now.
explain: Look for clues in the rest of the sentence. «Dinner is ready» means the food is finished and can be eaten. Try your meaning in the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
explain: Ready can also mean willing or likely. Look at what follows it. «Learning where the block, mute and report buttons are before trouble comes makes you ready» means you have a plan, so you are prepared if trouble comes.
fact: Ready means prepared and able to do something now.
fact: Ready can mean finished and able to be used or eaten, as in «dinner is ready».
fact: Ready to do something can mean willing or likely to do it.
fact: «Get ready» means prepare, such as dressing or packing your bag.
fact: Other forms of ready: readier, readiest (adjectives), readily (adverb), readiness (noun).
exercise: «So he has just 5 minutes to get ready.» What does get ready mean here? | Run a race | *Get your things and prepare to go | Pack the bag and dress, to prepare | Go to sleep
exercise: Which meaning of ready is used in «Dinner is ready»? | Willing to share | Happy and smiling | Late | *Finished and able to be eaten
exercise: «Learning where the block, mute and report buttons are before trouble comes makes you ready.» What does ready mean? | Angry and fast | Lost and confused | Finished forever | *Prepared, with a plan for trouble
test: Which word is the adverb form of ready? | readiness | *readily | readiest | readier
test: «You never kick or throw a ball at someone who is not ready.» What does ready mean here? | Loud and excited | *Prepared and willing to take a ball | Late for the game | Finished and cooked
exam: Which sentence uses ready to mean finished? | «Are you ready for school?» | «Get ready for the trip.» | «Ready, steady, go!» | *«Dinner is ready, so come and eat.»
